          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        Dermatology treats blackheads by clearing trapped oil and dead skin cells inside the follicle with lipid-soluble chemical exfoliants and topical retinoids, rather than by pulling them out with physical strips or aggressive squeezing.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Dermatologists recommend daily leave-on beta hydroxy acids, particularly salicylic acid, alongside topical retinoids to dissolve dead cells and sebum inside the pore and normalize cell turnover. Physical methods like pore strips, aggressive scrubs, and manual squeezing provide only temporary surface clearing while risking irritation, enlarged-looking pores, and dark marks on Indian skin. Start with a gentle cleanser and a low-percentage leave-on salicylic acid product two to three nights a week, follow with a lightweight moisturiser, and give the skin six to eight weeks of consistent care before expecting visible clearance.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Blackheads remain one of the most common skin concerns in India, prompting many to rely on nose strips, coarse scrubs, and magnifying mirrors. That mechanical approach only clears the surface tip for a few days. Lasting clearance requires treating the clog inside the follicle with ingredients that clinical dermatology actually supports.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What causes a blackhead (and why it turns dark)
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A blackhead is what dermatologists call an open comedo.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38300170/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a> It forms when excess oil produced by sebaceous glands mixes with shed dead skin cells inside the follicle lining. Instead of sloughing off smoothly, the cells clump together with sebum, forming a microscopic plug known as a microcomedone.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When the pore opening stays open to air, the plug oxidises. The dark surface dot is not dirt; it is melanin and sebum reacting with ambient oxygen, like a sliced apple browning on a counter.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26347269/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> Scrubbing cannot wash away oxidised lipids settled deep inside the follicle.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        It also helps to distinguish blackheads from sebaceous filaments. Sebaceous filaments are normal structures that guide oil along the pore wall, appearing as tiny, flat grey or yellow dots across the nose. Unlike blackheads, which are genuine plugs that stretch the pore, sebaceous filaments are normal anatomy and refill naturally within two days.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Why physical removal methods backfire
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Adhesive strips, peel-off masks, metal loops, and pore vacuums promise quick clearing. While they pull out the top of a plug, dermatologists advise against them because physical force damages skin in ways that worsen congestion over time.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Squeezing pores or pulling with adhesive strips puts shear stress on the follicle wall. If that wall breaks beneath the surface, trapped oil and bacteria leak into surrounding tissue, turning a blackhead into a painful red pimple.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38300170/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a> On Indian skin, mechanical trauma also stimulates pigment cells, leaving dark marks that take months to fade.
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Bruising and red marks.</strong> Strong suction and pinching can leave red marks and bruises.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Follicular rupture and inflammation.</strong> Downward pressure can break the pore canal beneath the surface, converting a quiet plug into a swollen blemish.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Lingering dark marks.</strong> In melanin-rich skin, friction triggers excess pigment production, leaving stubborn brown marks that outlast the clog.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Rapid recurrence.</strong> Strips pull out only the superficial horn while stripping barrier lipids, allowing the pore to refill with sebum within forty-eight hours.</li>
    </ul>

    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">
