    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            Pores do not have muscles, so they cannot open, close, or shrink on command. Real clearance comes from dissolving the buildup inside the pore canal with gentle acids, not from pulling plugs out mechanically.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What dermatology actually recommends
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Dermatology focuses on two proven pillars to keep pores clear: chemical exfoliation with beta hydroxy acids and cellular regulation with topical retinoids.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38300170/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Salicylic acid is the primary active for blackheads. Unlike water-soluble alpha hydroxy acids, salicylic acid is oil-soluble.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26347269/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> This property lets it penetrate through surface sebum straight into the pore canal. Once inside, it breaks the bonds holding dead skin cells together, allowing trapped debris to loosen and shed naturally.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26347269/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        In a 500-patient clinical trial for mild to moderate acne, a 2% salicylic acid hydrogel achieved comedone clearance comparable to adapalene gel over twelve weeks, with fewer adverse events.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38590107/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a>
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The second pillar is topical retinoids, such as over-the-counter adapalene or prescription tretinoin. Retinoids normalize how cells shed along the follicle lining, stopping microcomedones from forming before they become visible blackheads.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28585191/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a> For stubborn comedones that resist topical care, dermatologists perform sterile in-office extractions without tearing tissue.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        A comparison of blackhead removal methods
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Choosing the right method depends on your skin sensitivity, how dense the clogs are, and how well your barrier handles active ingredients.
    </p>
