    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        A dermatologist-aligned daily routine
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Consistency matters far more than strength when treating blackheads. An overly aggressive routine that strips your barrier triggers rebound oiliness, making pores look larger. Follow this simple four-step structure:
    </p>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Cleanse gently with lukewarm water.</strong> Use a mild gel cleanser for under a minute morning and night. Avoid coarse physical scrubs that irritate the pore rim.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Apply an oil-soluble active serum.</strong> Smooth 3 to 4 drops of a leave-on salicylic acid serum across slightly damp skin in the congested area, rather than dabbing single spots.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Hydrate with a light moisturiser.</strong> Use a light lotion or gel containing ceramides. Well-hydrated skin sheds cells smoothly and maintains elastic pore walls.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Protect with broad-spectrum sunscreen.</strong> Apply SPF 30 or higher every morning. Sunlight oxidises surface sebum, turning plugs darker and delaying mark fading.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Where a targeted serum fits in
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        <a href="/products/acne-blemish-control-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Acne, Blemish Control Serum</a>
        clears congestion inside the pore with 1% salicylic acid while glycolic acid smooths the surface. To prevent irritation on Indian skin, the formula balances its acids with niacinamide for less oil through the day and more even tone, Centella asiatica (cica) to calm the look of redness, and five ceramides with cholesterol to rebuild the barrier without a stripped feeling. Conditioned media supplies cell-derived signalling factors that help the actives work harder.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        In the serum's own clinical study on 36 Indian women, ages 18 to 30, with acne, comedones were down 61.6% in four weeks. Lesion count was down 26.2% at two weeks and 47.7% at four weeks, while sebum was down 19.4% in four weeks. Results may vary.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Apply 3 to 4 drops on damp skin after cleansing and before moisturiser. It is your only active; extra acids are not needed in the routine. Patch test first and start every other night if skin reacts easily. Because it contains salicylic acid, check with a doctor if pregnant or breastfeeding, and consult a dermatologist before combining with prescription tretinoin or clindamycin.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Common mistakes that make blackheads worse
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Even a well-planned skincare routine can run into trouble if everyday habits work against it. Avoid these common mistakes:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Over-washing and rough scrubbing.</strong> Washing multiple times daily or using walnut scrubs strips surface lipids, prompting skin to produce more oil.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Stacking multiple acid products.</strong> Combining an acid wash, toner, and serum damages the barrier and causes peeling without speeding clearance.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Skipping moisturiser on oily skin.</strong> Dehydrated skin sheds irregular flakes of dead cells that clump together, creating tighter clogs.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Treating blackheads as isolated spots.</strong> Dabbing acid only on visible dots ignores adjacent microcomedones. Apply serum across the entire oily zone.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Giving up before eight weeks.</strong> Pores need four to eight weeks of steady cell turnover to clear debris. Switching products weekly resets progress.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.8px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>
    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can you permanently shrink or close your pores?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No. Pore size depends largely on genetics and natural oil output. Pores have no muscles, so they cannot open or close. Keeping pores free of oxidised sebum prevents stretching, helping them appear smaller.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How long does salicylic acid take to clear blackheads?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Surface shine often reduces within one to two weeks, while noticeable clearance of blackheads typically takes four to eight weeks of steady care, matching natural skin cell turnover.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">What is the difference between blackheads and sebaceous filaments?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Sebaceous filaments are normal structures that line the pore to guide oil, appearing as flat, tiny grey or yellow dots. Blackheads are plugs of oxidised oil and dead cells that distend the pore.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Are blackheads caused by poor hygiene or trapped dirt?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No. The dark color is caused by melanin and sebum oxidising when exposed to air. Washing more than twice daily or scrubbing roughly only causes irritation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I use pore strips once a week if I moisturise afterwards?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Dermatologists advise against pore strips because they yank surface cells and strip the barrier while leaving the clog base behind, risking irritation and dark marks.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Should I apply a salicylic acid serum as a spot treatment or over the whole face?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Apply it across the entire oily or congestion-prone zone. Widespread application clears existing blackheads while stopping surrounding microcomedones from turning into new plugs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">When should I see a dermatologist?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Consult a dermatologist if blackheads persist after eight to twelve weeks of consistent care, if blemishes turn into painful cysts, or if previous squeezing has left scarring.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

